      "version": 311,
      "versionNonce": 91824731,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793001059638,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "[student@workstation monitoring-performance]$ ./request_inference_to_qwen3.sh 5 10\nSending 5 inference requests to https://qwen3-06b-monitoring-performance.apps.lab.example.com/v1/chat/completions\nInterval: 10s between requests (CPU model, be patient)\n\nRequest 1/5 - \"What is Kubernetes?\"\n  -> Kubernetes is an open-source system that automates and manages containerized applications. It provides a consistent and scalable way to deploy, scale, and maintain applications across multiple clusters. Key features include:\n\n- **Automated Scaling**: Automatically adjusts the number of replicas based on demand.\n- **Deployment Management**: Enables easy deployment, rollback, and updates to applications.\n- **Cluster Management**: Manages containers, services, and resources across a cluster.\n- **Orchestration**: Manages tasks and processes across multiple machines.\n- **Monitoring**: Tracks the health and performance of applications.\n- **Security**: Provides security features like authentication and access control.\n\nKubernetes is often used by developers to manage containerized applications in a production environment.\n\nRequest 2/5 - \"Explain containers in one sentence.\"\n  -> Containers are a way to organize and manage data in a program, allowing you to store, retrieve, and modify data in a structured manner.\n\nRequest 3/5 - \"What is OpenShift?\"\n  -> OpenShift is an open-source platform for container orchestration and management. It provides a scalable and flexible way to deploy, monitor, and manage applications across multiple containers in a cloud environment.\n\nKey features of OpenShift include:\n\n1. **Orchestration**: It automates the deployment, scaling, and management of containers across different environments.\n2. **Monitoring**: It offers tools for monitoring application performance and health.\n3. **CI/CD**: It supports continuous integration and continuous delivery.\n4. **Security**: It provides security features for containers and applications.\n5. **Management**: It allows users to manage their applications and infrastructure.\n\nOpenShift is built on top of Kubernetes and provides a simpler and more intuitive interface for deploying and managing applications. It is\n\nRequest 4/5 - \"Define machine learning briefly.\"\n  -> Machine learning is a subset of artificial intelligence that focuses on developing algorithms and models that can learn patterns from data rather than being explicitly programmed to perform specific tasks. The goal is to enable systems to make decisions or perform tasks with minimal human intervention.\n\nRequest 5/5 - \"What is model serving?\"\n  -> Model serving refers to the process of deploying and managing a model (such as a pre-trained language model) in a production environment. It involves:\n\n1. **Training and Preparing the Model**: Training a model and preparing it for use in a production setting.\n2. **Deploying the Model**: Deploying the trained model into a production system or service.\n3. **Monitoring and Maintaining the Model**: Monitoring the model's performance and making updates or adjustments as needed.\n\nIn machine learning and AI contexts, model serving is a key component of building scalable and efficient systems that can handle real-time or batch processing of data.\n\n\nDone. Check the vLLM Grafana dashboard for updated metrics.\nSelect 'qwen3-06b' in the model_name dropdown.",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "[student@workstation monitoring-performance]$ ./request_inference_to_qwen3.sh 5 10\nSending 5 inference requests to https://qwen3-06b-monitoring-performance.apps.lab.example.com/v1/chat/completions\nInterval: 10s between requests (CPU model, be patient)\n\nRequest 1/5 - \"What is Kubernetes?\"\n  -> Kubernetes is an open-source system that automates and manages containerized applications. It provides a consistent and scalable way to deploy, scale, and maintain applications across multiple clusters. Key features include:\n\n- **Automated Scaling**: Automatically adjusts the number of replicas based on demand.\n- **Deployment Management**: Enables easy deployment, rollback, and updates to applications.\n- **Cluster Management**: Manages containers, services, and resources across a cluster.\n- **Orchestration**: Manages tasks and processes across multiple machines.\n- **Monitoring**: Tracks the health and performance of applications.\n- **Security**: Provides security features like authentication and access control.\n\nKubernetes is often used by developers to manage containerized applications in a production environment.\n\nRequest 2/5 - \"Explain containers in one sentence.\"\n  -> Containers are a way to organize and manage data in a program, allowing you to store, retrieve, and modify data in a structured manner.\n\nRequest 3/5 - \"What is OpenShift?\"\n  -> OpenShift is an open-source platform for container orchestration and management. It provides a scalable and flexible way to deploy, monitor, and manage applications across multiple containers in a cloud environment.\n\nKey features of OpenShift include:\n\n1. **Orchestration**: It automates the deployment, scaling, and management of containers across different environments.\n2. **Monitoring**: It offers tools for monitoring application performance and health.\n3. **CI/CD**: It supports continuous integration and continuous delivery.\n4. **Security**: It provides security features for containers and applications.\n5. **Management**: It allows users to manage their applications and infrastructure.\n\nOpenShift is built on top of Kubernetes and provides a simpler and more intuitive interface for deploying and managing applications. It is\n\nRequest 4/5 - \"Define machine learning briefly.\"\n  -> Machine learning is a subset of artificial intelligence that focuses on developing algorithms and models that can learn patterns from data rather than being explicitly programmed to perform specific tasks. The goal is to enable systems to make decisions or perform tasks with minimal human intervention.\n\nRequest 5/5 - \"What is model serving?\"\n  -> Model serving refers to the process of deploying and managing a model (such as a pre-trained language model) in a production environment. It involves:\n\n1. **Training and Preparing the Model**: Training a model and preparing it for use in a production setting.\n2. **Deploying the Model**: Deploying the trained model into a production system or service.\n3. **Monitoring and Maintaining the Model**: Monitoring the model's performance and making updates or adjustments as needed.\n\nIn machine learning and AI contexts, model serving is a key component of building scalable and efficient systems that can handle real-time or batch processing of data.\n\n\nDone. Check the vLLM Grafana dashboard for updated metrics.\nSelect 'qwen3-06b' in the model_name dropdown.",
